  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":66,"t":"assess_parts","p":"At a market meeting, every trader shakes hands exactly once with every other trader. With 2, 3, 4 and 5 traders there are 1, 3, 6 and 10 handshakes. Explain why this pattern is quadratic, and write the rule for Hₙ.\\n\\nb How many handshakes take place at a meeting of 20 traders? Show your substitution.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"At a market meeting, every trader shakes hands exactly once with every other trader. With 2, 3, 4 and 5 traders there are 1, 3, 6 and 10 handshakes. Explain why this pattern is quadratic, and write the rule for Hₙ."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b How many handshakes take place at a meeting of 20 traders? Show your substitution."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Terms 1, 3, 6, 10. First differences: 2, 3, 4. Second difference: 1 — constant and non-zero, so the sequence is quadratic with leading coefficient 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5. Combinatorial argument: each handshake is a pair of traders; the number of pairs from n traders is n(n − 1) ÷ 2. Check: 2 → 1, 3 → 3, 4 → 6, 5 → 10 ✓. Rule: Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2 (= ½n² − ½n). (b) H₂₀ = 20 × 19 ÷ 2 = 380 ÷ 2 = 190 handshakes.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for first differences 2, 3, 4 and the constant second difference 1 with the quadratic conclusion, 1 for the combinatorial or coefficient argument, 1 for Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the substitution shown, 1 for 190.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — Hₙ = ½n² − ½n; ‘(n choose 2)’; 190 for any equivalent correct working.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":66,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Count the pairs; the double-count trick gives the factor 2.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) why quadratic + the rule","prompt":"Explain why this pattern is quadratic, and write the rule for Hₙ.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Compute the consecutive gaps, then the gap of the gaps.","Count pairs of people directly — each handshake is one unique pair."],"accepted":["differences 2, 3, 4; second difference 1 (constant) ⇒ quadratic; Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2","constant second difference 1; the number of pairs from n traders is n(n − 1)/2"],"keywords":[["second difference","second-difference"],["1"],["n(n - 1)","n(n−1)","n(n-1)/2","n(n − 1) ÷ 2","pairs","combination"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"b","label":"(b) handshakes with 20 traders","prompt":"How many handshakes take place at a meeting of 20 traders? Show your substitution.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Substitute n = 20 into your own rule.","The product of the two neighbours has a factor of one-half."],"answer":190,"tolerance":0,"unit":"handshakes"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Terms 1, 3, 6, 10. First differences: 2, 3, 4. Second difference: 1 — constant and non-zero, so the sequence is quadratic with leading coefficient 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5. Combinatorial argument: each handshake is a pair of traders; the number of pairs from n traders is n(n − 1) ÷ 2. Check: 2 → 1, 3 → 3, 4 → 6, 5 → 10 ✓. Rule: Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2 (= ½n² − ½n). (b) H₂₀ = 20 × 19 ÷ 2 = 380 ÷ 2 = 190 handshakes.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for first differences 2, 3, 4 and the constant second difference 1 with the quadratic conclusion, 1 for the combinatorial or coefficient argument, 1 for Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the substitution shown, 1 for 190.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — Hₙ = ½n² − ½n; ‘(n choose 2)’; 190 for any equivalent correct working.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Terms 1, 3, 6, 10. First differences: 2, 3, 4. Second difference: 1 — constant and non-zero, so the sequence is quadratic with leading coefficient 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5. Combinatorial argument: each handshake is a pair of traders; the number of pairs from n traders is n(n − 1) ÷ 2. Check: 2 → 1, 3 → 3, 4 → 6, 5 → 10 ✓. Rule: Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2 (= ½n² − ½n). (b) H₂₀ = 20 × 19 ÷ 2 = 380 ÷ 2 = 190 handshakes.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for first differences 2, 3, 4 and the constant second difference 1 with the quadratic conclusion, 1 for the combinatorial or coefficient argument, 1 for Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the substitution shown, 1 for 190.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — Hₙ = ½n² − ½n; ‘(n choose 2)’; 190 for any equivalent correct working.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Terms 1, 3, 6, 10. First differences: 2, 3, 4. Second difference: 1 — constant and non-zero, so the sequence is quadratic with leading coefficient 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5. Combinatorial argument: each handshake is a pair of traders; the number of pairs from n traders is n(n − 1) ÷ 2. Check: 2 → 1, 3 → 3, 4 → 6, 5 → 10 ✓. Rule: Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2 (= ½n² − ½n). (b) H₂₀ = 20 × 19 ÷ 2 = 380 ÷ 2 = 190 handshakes.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for first differences 2, 3, 4 and the constant second difference 1 with the quadratic conclusion, 1 for the combinatorial or coefficient argument, 1 for Hₙ = n(n − 1) ÷ 2. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the substitution shown, 1 for 190.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — Hₙ = ½n² − ½n; ‘(n choose 2)’; 190 for any equivalent correct working."},{"n":67,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A motorbike bought for 500 000 FCFA loses 10% of its value every year. Show that its value after n years is 500 000 × (0.9)ⁿ FCFA.\\n\\nb Find its value at the end of the 4th year, and the first year at the end of which its value is below 300 000 FCFA.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A motorbike bought for 500 000 FCFA loses 10% of its value every year. Show that its value after n years is 500 000 × (0.9)ⁿ FCFA."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Find its value at the end of the 4th year, and the first year at the end of which its value is below 300 000 FCFA."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Losing 10% means keeping 90% each year. value(0) = 500 000. value(1) = 500 000 × 0.9; value(2) = value(1) × 0.9 = 500 000 × 0.9²; continuing, after n years: value(n) = 500 000 × 0.9ⁿ. (b) V₄ = 500 000 × 0.6561 = 328 050 FCFA. Below 300 000 needs 0.9ⁿ < 0.6: n = 4 gives 0.6561 (not enough); n = 5 gives 0.59049 ✓ — value after 5 years = 500 000 × 0.59049 = 295 245 FCFA, the first year-end below 300 000 FCFA is year 5.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for ‘keeps 90% / multiply by 0.9 each year’, 1 for the exponent structure 0.9ⁿ (repeated multiplication or solving the recurrence). (b) 3 marks: 1 for V₄ = 328 050, 1 for testing 0.9⁴ and 0.9⁵ (or the inequality), 1 for ‘year 5’ with evidence.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘295 245 FCFA’ as the check for year 5; any consistent rounding of 328 050.","m":5,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":67,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Multiply by 0.9, count the years, test 0.9⁴ against 0.9⁵.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) show the decay rule","prompt":"Show that its value after n years is 500 000 × (0.9)ⁿ FCFA.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Each year the bike keeps 90% of its value — multiply by 0.9 repeatedly.","After 1 year: 500 000 × 0.9; × 0.9 again = × 0.9² after 2 years… so after n years: 500 000 × 0.9ⁿ."],"accepted":["each year the value is multiplied by 0.9 (10% loss ⇒ 90% remains); after n multiplications the value is 500 000 × 0.9ⁿ","value(n) = value(n−1) × 0.9 with value(0) = 500 000 — solving gives 500 000 × 0.9ⁿ"],"keywords":[["90%","0.9"],["each year","every year","multiply","×"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) value at the end of year 4","prompt":"Find its value at the end of the 4th year.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["V₄ = 500 000 × 0.9⁴.","Compute the fourth power of the retention factor, then multiply by the purchase price."],"answer":328050,"tolerance":100,"unit":"FCFA"},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) first year below 300 000 FCFA","prompt":"Find the first year at the end of which its value is below 300 000 FCFA.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Solve 500 000 × 0.9ⁿ < 300 000, i.e. 0.9ⁿ < 0.6.","Compare the fourth and fifth powers of 0.9 against 0.6; take the first year that passes."],"answer":5,"tolerance":0,"unit":"years"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Losing 10% means keeping 90% each year. value(0) = 500 000. value(1) = 500 000 × 0.9; value(2) = value(1) × 0.9 = 500 000 × 0.9²; continuing, after n years: value(n) = 500 000 × 0.9ⁿ. (b) V₄ = 500 000 × 0.6561 = 328 050 FCFA. Below 300 000 needs 0.9ⁿ < 0.6: n = 4 gives 0.6561 (not enough); n = 5 gives 0.59049 ✓ — value after 5 years = 500 000 × 0.59049 = 295 245 FCFA, the first year-end below 300 000 FCFA is year 5.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for ‘keeps 90% / multiply by 0.9 each year’, 1 for the exponent structure 0.9ⁿ (repeated multiplication or solving the recurrence). (b) 3 marks: 1 for V₄ = 328 050, 1 for testing 0.9⁴ and 0.9⁵ (or the inequality), 1 for ‘year 5’ with evidence.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘295 245 FCFA’ as the check for year 5; any consistent rounding of 328 050.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Losing 10% means keeping 90% each year. value(0) = 500 000. value(1) = 500 000 × 0.9; value(2) = value(1) × 0.9 = 500 000 × 0.9²; continuing, after n years: value(n) = 500 000 × 0.9ⁿ. (b) V₄ = 500 000 × 0.6561 = 328 050 FCFA. Below 300 000 needs 0.9ⁿ < 0.6: n = 4 gives 0.6561 (not enough); n = 5 gives 0.59049 ✓ — value after 5 years = 500 000 × 0.59049 = 295 245 FCFA, the first year-end below 300 000 FCFA is year 5.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for ‘keeps 90% / multiply by 0.9 each year’, 1 for the exponent structure 0.9ⁿ (repeated multiplication or solving the recurrence). (b) 3 marks: 1 for V₄ = 328 050, 1 for testing 0.9⁴ and 0.9⁵ (or the inequality), 1 for ‘year 5’ with evidence.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘295 245 FCFA’ as the check for year 5; any consistent rounding of 328 050.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Losing 10% means keeping 90% each year. value(0) = 500 000. value(1) = 500 000 × 0.9; value(2) = value(1) × 0.9 = 500 000 × 0.9²; continuing, after n years: value(n) = 500 000 × 0.9ⁿ. (b) V₄ = 500 000 × 0.6561 = 328 050 FCFA. Below 300 000 needs 0.9ⁿ < 0.6: n = 4 gives 0.6561 (not enough); n = 5 gives 0.59049 ✓ — value after 5 years = 500 000 × 0.59049 = 295 245 FCFA, the first year-end below 300 000 FCFA is year 5.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for ‘keeps 90% / multiply by 0.9 each year’, 1 for the exponent structure 0.9ⁿ (repeated multiplication or solving the recurrence). (b) 3 marks: 1 for V₄ = 328 050, 1 for testing 0.9⁴ and 0.9⁵ (or the inequality), 1 for ‘year 5’ with evidence.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘295 245 FCFA’ as the check for year 5; any consistent rounding of 328 050."},{"n":68,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A student says that the sequence 5, 10, 20, 40, … is linear because the terms are doubling steadily. Calculate the first differences and the ratios, and say whether the student is right.\\n\\nb Write the recursive and the explicit rule for the sequence, and explain the difference between a rule that adds and a rule that multiplies.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A student says that the sequence 5, 10, 20, 40, … is linear because the terms are doubling steadily. Calculate the first differences and the ratios, and say whether the student is right."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Write the recursive and the explicit rule for the sequence, and explain the difference between a rule that adds and a rule that multiplies."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Differences: 10 − 5 = 5; 20 − 10 = 10; 40 − 20 = 20 — not constant. Ratios: 10 ÷ 5 = 2; 20 ÷ 10 = 2; 40 ÷ 20 = 2 — constant. The student is wrong: doubling steadily is a constant multiplier, which defines a GEOMETRIC sequence, not a linear one (a linear sequence grows by the same amount each step). (b) Recursive: u₁ = 5, uₙ = 2 × uₙ₋₁ (equivalently uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ). Explicit: uₙ = 5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹. A rule that ADDS the same amount each time produces constant differences — an arithmetic (linear) sequence growing steadily by that amount. A rule that MULTIPLIES by the same factor produces constant ratios — a geometric sequence whose terms grow faster and faster (5, 10, 20, 40, …), which is why doubling ‘steadily’ is not ‘linear’ in the mathematical sense at all.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the correct differences and ratios shown, 1 for the verdict (not linear / geometric) with a one-line reason. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the recursive rule, 1 for uₙ = 5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹, 1 for the adds-vs-multiplies explanation (constant difference vs constant ratio; linear vs geometric).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘geometric’; explicit forms 5·2ⁿ⁻¹; explanations in either order as long as both cases are covered.","m":5,"df":"medium","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":68,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Divide, don’t subtract, when the multiplier is suspected.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) first differences and ratios","prompt":"Calculate the first differences and the ratios, and say whether the student is right.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compute the gaps AND the ratios between consecutive terms.","Doubling is an operation, not an addition — which kind of sequence is that?"],"accepted":["differences 5, 10, 20 (not constant); ratios 2, 2, 2 (constant) — the student is wrong; it is geometric, not linear","not linear — differences grow (5, 10, 20), ratios are constant 2, so it is geometric"],"keywords":[["5, 10, 20","5,10,20","5 10 20"],["2, 2, 2","2,2,2","ratio 2","×2"],["wrong","incorrect","not linear","geometric"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) recursive rule","prompt":"Write the recursive rule.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Note the first term.","Write the recurrence: next term as a multiple of the previous one."],"accepted":["u₁ = 5, uₙ = 2 × uₙ₋₁","u1 = 5, un = 2un-1","uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ, u₁ = 5","start at 5, double each time"],"keywords":[["u₁ = 5","u1 = 5","first term 5","starts at 5"],["2"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) explicit rule","prompt":"Write the explicit rule.","kind":"expression","points":1,"hints":["First term times the ratio raised to n − 1.","Test the rule against the printed terms."],"accepted":["uₙ = 5 × 2^(n-1)","un = 5*2^(n-1)","5*2^(n-1)","uₙ = 5·2ⁿ⁻¹","5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹","u(n) = 5*2^(n-1)"]},{"id":"b3","label":"(b) adds vs multiplies","prompt":"Explain the difference between a rule that adds and a rule that multiplies.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Adding the same amount each time gives a linear (arithmetic) sequence — constant differences.","Multiplying by the same factor gives a geometric sequence — constant ratios; the terms grow faster and faster."],"accepted":["adding a constant gives arithmetic (constant differences); multiplying by a constant gives geometric (constant ratios) — multiplication makes terms grow by an increasing amount each time","add = arithmetic/linear, difference constant; multiply = geometric, ratio constant"],"keywords":[["arithmetic","linear","constant difference"],["geometric","constant ratio"]],"minKeywordGroups":2}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Differences: 10 − 5 = 5; 20 − 10 = 10; 40 − 20 = 20 — not constant. Ratios: 10 ÷ 5 = 2; 20 ÷ 10 = 2; 40 ÷ 20 = 2 — constant. The student is wrong: doubling steadily is a constant multiplier, which defines a GEOMETRIC sequence, not a linear one (a linear sequence grows by the same amount each step). (b) Recursive: u₁ = 5, uₙ = 2 × uₙ₋₁ (equivalently uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ). Explicit: uₙ = 5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹. A rule that ADDS the same amount each time produces constant differences — an arithmetic (linear) sequence growing steadily by that amount. A rule that MULTIPLIES by the same factor produces constant ratios — a geometric sequence whose terms grow faster and faster (5, 10, 20, 40, …), which is why doubling ‘steadily’ is not ‘linear’ in the mathematical sense at all.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the correct differences and ratios shown, 1 for the verdict (not linear / geometric) with a one-line reason. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the recursive rule, 1 for uₙ = 5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹, 1 for the adds-vs-multiplies explanation (constant difference vs constant ratio; linear vs geometric).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘geometric’; explicit forms 5·2ⁿ⁻¹; explanations in either order as long as both cases are covered.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Differences: 10 − 5 = 5; 20 − 10 = 10; 40 − 20 = 20 — not constant. Ratios: 10 ÷ 5 = 2; 20 ÷ 10 = 2; 40 ÷ 20 = 2 — constant. The student is wrong: doubling steadily is a constant multiplier, which defines a GEOMETRIC sequence, not a linear one (a linear sequence grows by the same amount each step). (b) Recursive: u₁ = 5, uₙ = 2 × uₙ₋₁ (equivalently uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ). Explicit: uₙ = 5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹. A rule that ADDS the same amount each time produces constant differences — an arithmetic (linear) sequence growing steadily by that amount. A rule that MULTIPLIES by the same factor produces constant ratios — a geometric sequence whose terms grow faster and faster (5, 10, 20, 40, …), which is why doubling ‘steadily’ is not ‘linear’ in the mathematical sense at all.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the correct differences and ratios shown, 1 for the verdict (not linear / geometric) with a one-line reason. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the recursive rule, 1 for uₙ = 5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹, 1 for the adds-vs-multiplies explanation (constant difference vs constant ratio; linear vs geometric).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘geometric’; explicit forms 5·2ⁿ⁻¹; explanations in either order as long as both cases are covered.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Differences: 10 − 5 = 5; 20 − 10 = 10; 40 − 20 = 20 — not constant. Ratios: 10 ÷ 5 = 2; 20 ÷ 10 = 2; 40 ÷ 20 = 2 — constant. The student is wrong: doubling steadily is a constant multiplier, which defines a GEOMETRIC sequence, not a linear one (a linear sequence grows by the same amount each step). (b) Recursive: u₁ = 5, uₙ = 2 × uₙ₋₁ (equivalently uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ). Explicit: uₙ = 5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹. A rule that ADDS the same amount each time produces constant differences — an arithmetic (linear) sequence growing steadily by that amount. A rule that MULTIPLIES by the same factor produces constant ratios — a geometric sequence whose terms grow faster and faster (5, 10, 20, 40, …), which is why doubling ‘steadily’ is not ‘linear’ in the mathematical sense at all.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the correct differences and ratios shown, 1 for the verdict (not linear / geometric) with a one-line reason. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the recursive rule, 1 for uₙ = 5 × 2ⁿ⁻¹, 1 for the adds-vs-multiplies explanation (constant difference vs constant ratio; linear vs geometric).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘geometric’; explicit forms 5·2ⁿ⁻¹; explanations in either order as long as both cases are covered."},{"n":69,"t":"assess_parts","p":"The table shows the number of bacteria in a culture, which doubles every hour. Complete the row of ratios and state what the constant ratio tells you.\\n\\nb Write the explicit rule for the number of bacteria after n hours, and use it to find the number after 6 hours.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The table shows the number of bacteria in a culture, which doubles every hour. Complete the row of ratios and state what the constant ratio tells you."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Write the explicit rule for the number of bacteria after n hours, and use it to find the number after 6 hours."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"hours n"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"bacteria"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"300"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"600"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 200"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2 400"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) Ratios: 600 ÷ 300 = 2; 1200 ÷ 600 = 2; 2400 ÷ 1200 = 2 — the constant ratio is 2: the population doubles every hour, i.e. it is a geometric sequence with common ratio 2 (starting from 300 at hour 0). (b) Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ (hour 0 → 300, hour 1 → 600, …). After 6 hours: B₆ = 300 × 2⁶ = 300 × 64 = 19 200 bacteria.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the ratios 2, 2, 2 shown, 1 for interpreting them (doubling each hour / geometric with r = 2). (b) 3 marks: 1 for Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ, 2 for 19 200 after 6 hours (1 for the substitution, 1 for the value with units ‘bacteria’).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘19 200’; ‘B(n) = 300·2ⁿ’; zero-hour convention clear.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":69,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Divide each hour by the previous; then 300 × 2ⁿ.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a) ratios row","prompt":"Complete the row of ratios and state what the constant ratio tells you.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare each term with the one before it — constant gap, or constant multiplier?","A multiplier that never changes means each hour’s growth is proportional to what is already there."],"accepted":["ratios 2, 2, 2; constant ratio 2 — the culture doubles every hour, so it is geometric with common ratio 2","2, 2, 2 — doubling every hour (geometric, ratio 2)"],"keywords":[["2, 2, 2","2,2,2","2 2 2"],["double","doubling"],["ratio","geometric"]],"minKeywordGroups":3},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) explicit rule","prompt":"Write the explicit rule for the number of bacteria after n hours.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["Initial value 300.","Exponent equals the number of hours elapsed."],"accepted":["Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ","Bn = 300*2^n","B(n) = 300 × 2ⁿ","300 × 2ⁿ","300*2^n"],"keywords":[["300"],["2"]],"minKeywordGroups":2},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) bacteria after 6 hours","prompt":"Find the number of bacteria after 6 hours.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["B₆ = 300 × 2⁶.","Evaluate the power first, then multiply by the initial value."],"answer":19200,"tolerance":0,"unit":"bacteria"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) Ratios: 600 ÷ 300 = 2; 1200 ÷ 600 = 2; 2400 ÷ 1200 = 2 — the constant ratio is 2: the population doubles every hour, i.e. it is a geometric sequence with common ratio 2 (starting from 300 at hour 0). (b) Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ (hour 0 → 300, hour 1 → 600, …). After 6 hours: B₆ = 300 × 2⁶ = 300 × 64 = 19 200 bacteria.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the ratios 2, 2, 2 shown, 1 for interpreting them (doubling each hour / geometric with r = 2). (b) 3 marks: 1 for Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ, 2 for 19 200 after 6 hours (1 for the substitution, 1 for the value with units ‘bacteria’).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘19 200’; ‘B(n) = 300·2ⁿ’; zero-hour convention clear.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) Ratios: 600 ÷ 300 = 2; 1200 ÷ 600 = 2; 2400 ÷ 1200 = 2 — the constant ratio is 2: the population doubles every hour, i.e. it is a geometric sequence with common ratio 2 (starting from 300 at hour 0). (b) Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ (hour 0 → 300, hour 1 → 600, …). After 6 hours: B₆ = 300 × 2⁶ = 300 × 64 = 19 200 bacteria.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the ratios 2, 2, 2 shown, 1 for interpreting them (doubling each hour / geometric with r = 2). (b) 3 marks: 1 for Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ, 2 for 19 200 after 6 hours (1 for the substitution, 1 for the value with units ‘bacteria’).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘19 200’; ‘B(n) = 300·2ⁿ’; zero-hour convention clear.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","table","data_table"],"response_requirement":"any","response_guidance":""},"marking":"Model answer — (a) Ratios: 600 ÷ 300 = 2; 1200 ÷ 600 = 2; 2400 ÷ 1200 = 2 — the constant ratio is 2: the population doubles every hour, i.e. it is a geometric sequence with common ratio 2 (starting from 300 at hour 0). (b) Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ (hour 0 → 300, hour 1 → 600, …). After 6 hours: B₆ = 300 × 2⁶ = 300 × 64 = 19 200 bacteria.\\n\\nMarking — (a) 2 marks: 1 for the ratios 2, 2, 2 shown, 1 for interpreting them (doubling each hour / geometric with r = 2). (b) 3 marks: 1 for Bₙ = 300 × 2ⁿ, 2 for 19 200 after 6 hours (1 for the substitution, 1 for the value with units ‘bacteria’).\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘19 200’; ‘B(n) = 300·2ⁿ’; zero-hour convention clear."},{"n":70,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A geometric sequence has u₂ = 12 and u₅ = 324. Find the common ratio and the first term.\\n\\nb Write the explicit rule, and find which term is equal to 26 244.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A geometric sequence has u₂ = 12 and u₅ = 324. Find the common ratio and the first term."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"b Write the explicit rule, and find which term is equal to 26 244."}]}]},"e":"Model answer — (a) u₅ ÷ u₂ = r³: 324 ÷ 12 = 27 → r = 3. u₁ = u₂ ÷ r = 12 ÷ 3 = 4. (b) uₙ = 4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹. Setting uₙ = 26 244: 4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 26 244 → 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 6561 = 3⁸ → n − 1 = 8 → n = 9. The 9th term is 26 244 (check: 4 × 3⁸ = 4 × 6561 = 26 244 ✓).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for r³ = 27 with the division shown, 1 for r = 3, 1 for u₁ = 4. (b) 2 marks: 1 for uₙ = 4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹, 1 for n = 9 with the equation solved.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘r = 3, u₁ = 4’; ‘9th term’; rule written 4·3ⁿ⁻¹.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Geometric Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":70,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Divide u₅ by u₂ — that is r cubed.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","parts":[{"id":"a1","label":"(a) common ratio","prompt":"Find the common ratio.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["u₅ ÷ u₂ = 324 ÷ 12 covers three multiplies, r³.","Take the cube root of both sides."],"answer":3,"tolerance":0},{"id":"a2","label":"(a) first term","prompt":"Find the first term.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["u₂ = u₁ × r: divide the second term by your ratio.","That gives the first term."],"answer":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b1","label":"(b) explicit rule","prompt":"Write the explicit rule.","kind":"expression","points":1,"hints":["First term 4, ratio 3, exponent n − 1.","Test the rule against u₂ and u₅."],"accepted":["uₙ = 4 × 3^(n-1)","un = 4*3^(n-1)","4*3^(n-1)","uₙ = 4·3ⁿ⁻¹","4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹","u(n) = 4*3^(n-1)"]},{"id":"b2","label":"(b) which term equals 26 244","prompt":"Find which term is equal to 26 244.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide 26 244 by the first term.","Write the result as a power of 3 and read off n − 1."],"answer":9,"tolerance":0,"unit":"th term"}],"marking":"Model answer — (a) u₅ ÷ u₂ = r³: 324 ÷ 12 = 27 → r = 3. u₁ = u₂ ÷ r = 12 ÷ 3 = 4. (b) uₙ = 4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹. Setting uₙ = 26 244: 4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 26 244 → 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 6561 = 3⁸ → n − 1 = 8 → n = 9. The 9th term is 26 244 (check: 4 × 3⁸ = 4 × 6561 = 26 244 ✓).\\n\\nMarking — (a) 3 marks: 1 for r³ = 27 with the division shown, 1 for r = 3, 1 for u₁ = 4. (b) 2 marks: 1 for uₙ = 4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹, 1 for n = 9 with the equation solved.\\n\\nAcceptable alternates — ‘r = 3, u₁ = 4’; ‘9th term’; rule written 4·3ⁿ⁻¹.","marking_criteria":"Model answer — (a) u₅ ÷ u₂ = r³: 324 ÷ 12 = 27 → r = 3. u₁ = u₂ ÷ r = 12 ÷ 3 = 4. (b) uₙ = 4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹. Setting uₙ = 26 244: 4 × 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 26 244 → 3ⁿ⁻¹ = 6561 = 3⁸ → 